00:18:19.495 --> 00:18:23.298
<v Daniel>axioms, except for one consequence for determinantal complexity.</v>

155
00:18:23.587 --> 00:18:24.928
<v Maya>Wow, so they were thorough.</v>

156
00:18:25.288 --> 00:18:37.995
<v Daniel>Very. They even use a second independent kernel called NanoDA via a tool called Comparator for continuous integration rechecks. So they are immensely confident in the notary public.</v>

157
00:18:38.235 --> 00:18:49.121
<v Maya>But Empiropic's own readme comes with a glaring warning. It literally says, and I'm quoting here, Lean checks the proofs, but no machine checks that this file says what is claimed.</v>

158
00:18:49.613 --> 00:19:02.543
<v Daniel>They are actively warning the reader about statement fidelity. And this connects directly back to what we notice with OpenAI. OpenAI formalized a weaker statement than their abstract claimed. The machine can only verify exactly what it's fed.</v>

159
00:19:02.683 --> 00:19:17.132
<v Maya>Which is the terrifying part. As of November 25th, the day after these papers dropped, there are zero human expert reviews reported. None. The press coverage so far is limited entirely to tech outlets, not peer-reviewed mathematical journals. We have two</v>

160
00:19:17.352 --> 00:19:22.514
<v Maya>claimed proofs, but no human mathematician has verified either paper. So what does this all mean?</v>

161
00:19:23.134 --> 00:19:36.898
<v Daniel>It really forces us to ask, what happens to mathematical discovery when formal, verified proofs exist before any human actually understands them? Like, who is going to check these 80-page papers? And how fast can they realistically do it?</v>

162
00:19:37.233 --> 00:19:51.963
<v Maya>Right. We started this deep dive talking about the sheer, terrifying speed of AI discovery. Now we are staring at hundreds of pages of verified, logical code that claims to prove a foundational computer science conjecture. But the logic uses</v>

163
00:19:52.083 --> 00:19:59.969
<v Maya>non-explicit objects like Zorn's lemma, and it creates entire geometric universes that no human has actually conceptually parsed yet.</v>

164
00:20:00.407 --> 00:20:07.713
<v Daniel>The landscape has permanently shifted. I mean, the bottleneck is no longer generating the proof. The bottleneck is human comprehension.</v>

165
00:20:08.166 --> 00:20:22.975
<v Maya>I want to leave you, the listener, with a final thought to mull over. If AI models can now synthesize entirely new mathematical architectures, you know, bending geometry to solve algebra to bypass the very barriers that have stumped humans for decades, is the</v>

166
00:20:23.035 --> 00:20:37.825
<v Maya>future of mathematics really about humans writing proofs anymore? Or is it about humans becoming mere auditors of statement fidelity for alien machines that think in ways we can barely even comprehend? Keep an ear out, because the era of</v>

167
00:20:37.926 --> 00:20:40.550
<v Maya>human-only math might have just quietly closed.</v>
